Records the hubs and workers a binary leaves in the state directory, as a newer binary meets them (`tests/state_records.rs`).

Hubs and workers keep running across `adj server restart`, so the binary that reads a record is
not always the one that wrote it. One file per record:

    worker.json          <worktree>/.claude/adjutant-worker.json         a worker's record
    hub.json             <state>/hubs/<slug>.json                        a hub's record
    inbox-report.md      <state>/inbox/<slug>/<stamp>-report.md          a message waiting for the hub
    inbox-held.md        <state>/inbox/<slug>/.acking-<pid>-0-<name>     a message an ack was holding
    session-hub.json     <state>/sessions/<slug>.json                    the session a hub resumes from
    session-worker.json  <worktree>/.claude/adjutant-session.json        the session a worker resumes from
    gate.json            <state>/gates/<slug>/<id>.json                  an open gate (a plan)
    task.json            <state>/tasks/<slug>/<id>.json                  a task in the backlog

The tests write each one under the name in the second column, which is not the fixture's own.
The worker fixture names another hub, so it is kept out of the tests that read the repository's
own inbox, hub and sessions: its record would send them to a different slug.

Captured from the binary at 0.4.0 (main at eb21ee9): a scratch repository with origin
git@github.com:acme/widget.git, a scratch state directory, and these commands.

    adj hub                                                      hub.json, session-hub.json
    adj worker --worktree <repo> --title ... --hub wid-957 --task <id>
    adj phase --set implement --worktree <repo>                  worker.json, session-worker.json
    adj send --kind report --subject ... --body ... --hub wid-957    inbox-report.md
    adj task add --title ... --body ... --issue-url ... --json
    adj task update --id <id> --note ...                         task.json
    adj gate open --file payload.json --json                     gate.json

The runners were `true`, so no agent started. inbox-held.md is the same shape as the report
under the name `hold` gives a message while it is being acked: no command leaves one durably
(an ack holds it for two moves), so it is the report's format with a different message, named by hand.

Editing applied, since the repository is public: pids became 4242, timestamps, the stamps in
ids, session ids and the epoch seconds became fixed values, `psStarted` was written in the C
locale's words, paths became `/path/to/...`, and the sender and subject text was made plain.
Each file keeps the keys and nesting the binary wrote. The worker's `terminal` is what the
binary wrote outside tmux (`backend` alone); a record from inside tmux carries more.

Added by hand: an `x-unknown` key in each JSON record and as a header in each message. It stands
for a key a later version adds. It cannot be captured: `task add` and `gate open` clear
unknown keys, so the binary never writes one. The tests check it survives where a command
saves the record again (phase, gate close, task update, a gate closing onto its task) and is
shown where one only reads it (gate show/list, task show/list).

Not every record is written back. A hub's record and the sessions are only read here, so their
tests check the file is as it was afterwards. Some writers drop unknown keys on purpose: a
worker's start rewrites the record and session, a hub's start rewrites its own, linking a worker
to another hub on the board rewrites its session (the record keeps them), and `task add` and
`gate open` start from nothing. Nested objects (`terminal`, a gate's choices) have no catch-all
for unknown keys.

When the shape of a record changes, capture it again the same way rather than editing the
files by hand.
